WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.200
Let's take a look inside a typical server.
We'll focus on 2U, 2-socket

2
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.240
servers. They're most common in data
centers today. 2U

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.220
refers to the server's height in rack
units. It's roughly

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.020
3.5 inches tall, while 2-socket means it
can hold two

5
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.840
CPUs or processors. These processors work

6
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.840
together to handle high-performance tasks.
Inside the

7
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.660
server, we'll find several key components:
the CPU, the heat

8
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.120
sink, memory modules, PCIe cards for
expansion,

9
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.500
a power supply unit, hard drives or SSDs,
and multiple

10
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.240
cooling fans. These components all work

11
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.260
together to process, store, and transmit
data across the network.

12
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.460
The CPU, or processor, is the brain of the
server.

13
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.680
It performs the calculations and
instructions that drive applications.

14
00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.780
It's paired with a heat sink and fans to
manage the heat it generates.

15
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.940
Memory, or RAM, stores active data so the
CPU

16
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.540
can quickly access it. Servers also
include

17
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.680
PCIe expansion slots, which connect to the
PCIe

18
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.840
bus. It's a high-speed pathway that allows
the server to add extra

19
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.780
capabilities. This might include GPUs

20
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.860
for artificial intelligence processing,
network interface cards,

21
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.260
or high-performance sto- storage
controllers. These

22
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.720
components dramatically increase a
server's flexibility and

23
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.640
performance in specialized workloads.
Servers also

24
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.040
need fast, reliable storage. Hard drives
and

25
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.080
solid-state drives, or SSDs, are used
depending on the performance

26
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.180
needs. SSDs offer faster

27
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.860
access times and better durability, making
them ideal for

28
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.680
high-transaction environments. Traditional
spindle hard

29
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.680
drives offer more capacity for a lower
price but have lower performance

30
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.440
and require more power. And because they
have mechanical parts,

31
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.800
they are more likely to fail faster than
SSDs.

32
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.980
Of course, none of this would work without
power. Power

33
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.880
supply is a critical component. It
converts electrical current from the

34
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.820
rack to usable power for the server's
components. It's

35
00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.400
connected to a power distribution unit,
and it has redundant

36
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.560
feeds, and there's even a redundant power
supply in a system

37
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.480
to ensure reliability. Cooling is another
critical

38
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.400
part of the IT infrastructure. Air flows
from the cold

39
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.660
aisle, typically through perforated tiles
in the floor in front of the

40
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.560
server, and it passes over the hot
components and

41
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.740
exits as warm air out the back. This
airflow

42
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.040
ensures components stay within optimal
temperature range.

43
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.340
Beyond servers, data centers rely on other
infrastructure.

44
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.580
Networking equipment like routers and
switches connect servers and

45
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.740
devices across racks and even the
